<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emma Goldman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emma Goldman was a Russian-born anarchist revolutionary, political activist and writer who did much to develop anarchist political philosophy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> to a Lithuanian Jewish family, she immigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1885 and lectured on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>, women's rights and social issues; with Alexander Berkman she planned the 1892 attempt on the life of Henry Clay Frick. She founded the journal Mother Earth in 1906, was jailed in 1917 for conspiring against the draft and was deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in December 1919. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronstadt-rebellion">Kronstadt rebellion</a> she denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and later supported the anarchist revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emma-goldman.jpg" alt="Emma Goldman" width="330" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Takuma Kajiwara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emma_Goldman_seated.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Emma Goldman was born into an Orthodox Jewish family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>, then within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. The family later moved to the village of Papilė, where her father ran an inn. There she saw a peasant whipped with a knout in the street, an event that traumatised her and contributed to her lifelong distaste for violent authority.</p><p>In 1885 her sister Helena planned to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to join their sister Lena. Their father refused to let Emma go, and he agreed only after she threatened to throw herself into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva River</a>. She arrived at Castle Garden on 29 December 1885. On her first day she met Alexander Berkman, and heard Johann Most, the editor of Freiheit and an advocate of propaganda of the deed, who trained her in public speaking. She was drawn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a> after the persecution that followed the Haymarket affair.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Homestead Strike of 1892, Goldman and Berkman planned the killing of Henry Clay Frick, the manager of the Carnegie steel plant, as an act of propaganda of the deed. Frick survived and Berkman was sentenced to 22 years in prison. During the Panic of 1893 she spoke to a crowd of nearly 3,000 people in Union Square, and was imprisoned for inciting to riot.</p><p>After Leon Czolgosz shot President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley">William McKinley</a> in 1901, he said he had been inspired by a speech of hers, and she was arrested with twelve other anarchists. From 1903 she withdrew from public life and nursed, until opposition to the Anarchist Exclusion Act pulled her back. She founded Mother Earth in 1906, lectured across the country and was jailed for distributing information about birth control. In 1917 she and Berkman were sentenced to two years for conspiring to induce persons not to register for the draft. In the Red Scare they were arrested and deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in December 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Goldman first welcomed the Bolshevik revolution, but she grew alarmed and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronstadt-rebellion">Kronstadt rebellion</a>, denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> for its violent repression of independent voices. She left the Soviet Union and published My Disillusionment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1923, and lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, writing her autobiography Living My Life. In 1936 she went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> to support the anarchist revolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, where she edited the CNT-FAI Information Bulletin. She suffered a stroke on 17 February 1940 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> on 14 May 1940, aged 70.</p><p>In her lifetime admirers called her a freethinking rebel woman, and detractors called her an advocate of politically motivated murder. After decades of obscurity, she was read widely again in the 1970s, when feminist and anarchist scholars revived interest in her.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emma Goldman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Emma Goldman</b> and Errico Malatesta held violence to be a reaction to state violence, a necessary evil.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Anarchists such as <b>Emma Goldman</b> and Dwight Macdonald, who left the American Socialist Workers Party, raised Trotsky's part in the Kronstadt repression.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/trotskyism">Trotskyism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.9,23.93&amp;year=1915" aria-label="Kaunas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5076 286 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emma Goldman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5526 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5526 556h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.9,23.93&amp;year=1915">What was here in 1915? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
